### Justification
The claim is not universally true, as multiple U.S. states—including Ohio, Washington, and Pennsylvania—accepted and counted mail-in ballots postmarked by Election Day even if received afterward, as confirmed by [official state rules and court decisions](https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/addybaird/vote-by-mail-state-deadlines). There was [no federal law](https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot-deadlines/) mandating a uniform deadline, and states had authority to set varying rules, with some implementing [temporary extensions due to the COVID-19 pandemic](https://www.npr.org/2020/09/14/909338758/map-mail-in-voting-rules-by-state). While the claim accurately described the rules for specific jurisdictions like Michigan, presenting it as a general absolute rule omits this critical context, making it misleading by excluding widespread exceptions.